 Observability Low-Level Design (LLD)

This section is what engineers use to actually build observability in real projects.

 1. Data Collection Layer
Metrics

Node Exporter → CPU, RAM, Disk, Network

Application Metrics → Latency, Error rates, RPS

Prometheus scrapes exporters every X seconds

Metrics stored in time-series DB

Example metrics include:

http_request_duration_seconds

cpu_usage_total

memory_usage_bytes

Logs

Application logs (structured JSON recommended)

Access logs (Nginx, Apache)

System logs (syslog, journald)

Log collectors:

FluentD / FluentBit

Logstash

Splunk forwarder

OpenTelemetry Collector

Logs forwarded to:

ElasticSearch

Cloud Logging (GCP)

DataDog Logs

Splunk

Traces

Use OpenTelemetry instrumentation inside code

Each request generates:

Trace ID

Span ID

Parent ID

Tracing systems:

Jaeger

Zipkin

DataDog APM

OpenTelemetry Collector + Cloud Trace

 2. Storage Layer

Where telemetry is stored:

Data Type	Storage
Metrics	Prometheus / Thanos / Mimir
Logs	ElasticSearch / Cloud Logging
Traces	Jaeger / Tempo / DataDog
 3. Visualization Layer

Dashboards created for:

Latency (p50/p90/p99)

Error rates

CPU / Memory usage

API Traffic

Database performance

Trace waterfalls

Tools:

Grafana

Kibana

DataDog dashboard

New Relic One

4. Alerting Layer

Alerts built using:

Prometheus Alertmanager

DataDog monitors

NewRelic Alerts

Cloud Monitoring Alerts

Example alert rules:

groups:
- name: high_latency
  rules:
  - alert: HighRequestLatency
    expr: http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{le="0.5"} < 0.8
    for: 5m
    labels:
      severity: warning
    annotations:
      description: "Latency above 500ms"


Alerts trigger notifications:

Slack

Email

SMS

PagerDuty

